Experiment 1: Initialize a Git Repository

Aim

To initialize a local Git repository and track files using Git commands.

Requirements

- Git installed on the computer
- Command Prompt or VS Code terminal
- GitHub account

Procedure

1. Create a project folder.
2. Open the terminal inside the folder.
3. Initialize a Git repository.
4. Create a file named "index.txt".
5. Check the repository status.
6. Add the file to the staging area.
7. Commit the file to save the changes.

Commands

mkdir GitLab
cd GitLab
git init
echo "Hello GitHub" > index.txt
git status
git add index.txt
git commit -m "First commit"

Explanation of Commands

- "mkdir GitLab" – Creates a folder named GitLab.
- "cd GitLab" – Opens the folder.
- "git init" – Initializes a local Git repository.
- "echo "Hello GitHub" > index.txt" – Creates a text file with content.
- "git status" – Displays the status of files.
- "git add index.txt" – Adds the file to the staging area.
- "git commit -m "First commit"" – Saves the staged changes in Git.

Expected Output

Git initializes an empty repository, and the file "index.txt" is added and committed successfully.

Result

Thus, a local Git repository was initialized successfully, and a file was tracked and committed using Git commands.

Important Note

